                <head>80. Editorial Note</head>
                <p>On February 22, 1962, Secretary of State Rusk presented an address at Davidson College in Davidson, North
                    Carolina, in which he spoke of United States interests in South Vietnam. He
                    stated that “the United States has no national requirements in that area.”
                    Rather, he said, United States interests lay in preventing external aggression
                    from the north from succeeding. For text of the address, see Department of State
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Bulletin</hi>, March 19, 1962, pages 448-454.</p>
            </div>
